# Testing

Differential tests under `crisp_tests/` compare the original, just-transpiled Rust implementation with the current Rust implementation to detect behavior changes. Test generation is handled by a separate step; you are not required to add tests before refactoring. You may add, remove, or modify tests and supporting files under `crisp_tests/` when appropriate, including fixing invalid or flaky generated tests. Keep tests meaningful and preserve coverage of externally-visible behavior.


# Validation

{{ after_refactoring_instruction }}


Your changes must not introduce new unsafe code within implementation functions. You can check your work using this command:
```sh
crisp_check_unsafe2.sh
```
This will report an error for any unsafe code that was improperly added during your edits. It also reports errors on any newly added "unsafe-adjacent" code, including int-to-pointer casts and arguments or fields of raw pointer type.


Your changes also must not add excessive logic or complexity to FFI entry point functions (those with `#[no_mangle]` or `#[export_name = "..."]`).  All implementation logic must remain within non-FFI functions.  Check your work using this command:
```sh
crisp_ffi_review.sh
```
This will report an error if implementation code was improperly added to FFI functions.


Note: you MUST ensure that all three checks pass on your final version of the code before you stop.  If one check initially fails and you edit the code to fix it, be sure to re-run the other checks afterward to ensure that the change didn't break something else.


# Blockers

If, after making a serious attempt, you determine that it's not possible to complete the current goals while also satisfying the validation rules, proceed as follows:

1. If you can, make partial progress toward the goal while satisfying all of the mandatory validation checks.  A change that reduces the goal metric from 50 to 0 but fails a check will be discarded by the CRISP harness, resulting in no progress whatsoever.  A change that reduces the metric to 5 (not meeting the goal) but passes all checks will be accepted by the CRISP harness, and the harness will run a followup agent to try to address any remaining work.
2. If there's no way to make any progress at all toward the goal (while simultaneously satisfying the mandatory checks), create a file `{{ cargo_dir_path }}/BLOCKERS.md` containing a 1-2 paragraph description of the problem(s).  The CRISP harness will mark the current step as impossible and pass your blocker description to the planning agent so it can develop a new strategy.
